A week or two ago someone asked for early-game strategies to survive
the monsters and starvation long enough to get at least a level 6 or
7 character.  Some suggestions were given.  Here is another strategy
that I have been using and it seems to've done well for me so far.

spoiler alert...

Yes, there are lots of spoilers following, imbedded in the
strategy discussion.  If you quote me, please remove the ">"
from in front of the "^L"...

First you need to have ESP so you can see what you are doing after
you get blinded while using this strategy  ;^).  Eat a dead floating
eye (I always let my pet kill them for me.  If I am right there when
it dies I always get 1 turn to pick it up before my pet eats it - assuming
it leaves a corpse).

Second, you need a decent armor class (at least 3 or 4) and a decent
weapon.  I waited until I found a 2-handed sword before I started this
time.

Third, find a magic trap in as low a level as possible - ie level 3 or
4 (not always possible - if you can't find one then you can't use this
strategy).

Fourth - eat a tengu for teleportitous *after* you find the magic trap.
Teleporting uses up your food, so you need a source for food before you
start popping around - this strategy will provide you with lots of food,
but you could starve before you find a magic trap if you begin teleporting
first.

Now, step on the magic trap.  The majority of the time what happens is
three or four monsters are created surrounding you and you are blinded.
If you have ESP you can still see them.  Kill them and collect their
belongings.  Eat them if they are good to eat.  If they are not good to
eat then throw them against the far wall so you won't keep picking them
up by accident.  Take time to heal from your wounds.  Then step on the
trap again.  This technique can rapidly advance you to a level ten or
higher character.  It also can provide you with a significant stockpile
of food.

Dangers:

Occassionally you will get zapped by a "tower of flame" which will permanently
take away 3 or 4 hit points.  I always gain hit points from advancing levels
faster than I loose them, at least early on.  This is not nearly as frequent
as other results from this trap.

Occassionally instead of 3 or 4 random monsters you will get a whole room
full of orcs.  I have seen what appeared to be 20 orcs appear at the same
time (I didn't count them ;^).)  You need to be able to teleport away if
your low-level character is surrounded and getting whaled on from 8
different directions.

Note - if I am not too strong I will leave some acid blobs alive, as they
will surround me without attacking, and keep some of the dangerous monsters
from ganging up on me too much.

Sometimes your pack shakes violently.  I am not sure if this means anything.
I think it could mean something is getting cursed or broken or dis-enchanted,
but I haven't noticed anything actually being effected.

Other features:

Other possible results; you can gain charisma (this seems to happen often)
- I seem to be so charismatic now that a leprechaun befriended me for no
apparent reason - you yearn for your distant homeland, you hear howling,
shivers run up and down your spine, you feel like someone is helping you
(remove curse).  I don't know of any negative effects from any of these
messages (unless you were saving that cursed scroll of genocide to call
up a squadren of nurses...).

I love it when all the orcs appear; once your character is strong enough
they are not a threat, but in a room full of them there is often a wand
or potion or ring or food.  Once I load up with stuff I go down to the next
lower level and drop it in a stock-pile and return.  I only stay on the
level with all the stuff stored for a couple of turns (trying to lower
the chances that tunneling monsters will appear and destroy things).
You can quickly build a stockpile of 20 or 30 food rations on the lower
level.  If you find an identify scroll then go down and drop all your ID'ed
stuff into the pile & pick up one item each of the unknown stuff.  etc.

You can regain some of your permanently lost hit points by praying or
quaffing potions of extra healing.  Nurses can also be helpful here.
The major advantage of this strategy is that it provides you with lots
of monsters to fight in a row so you can gain those experience levels
faster.  The other advantage is that they bring you lots of interesting
items and you get plaenty of food from them.  The hit point loss can be
regained by other means after your character has advanced sufficiently
in levels.

I've been using this strategy on 2.3, so if magic traps are more dangerous
now then there could easily be more caveats.  I'm looking for the PL8
binary for my Amiga!  ;^)

